        { kind: "discovery", md: `As the chunks get thinner, the constant-velocity assumption gets better in each one (over a tiny sliver of time, velocity barely changes), and the sum of (velocity)$\\times$(tiny duration) converges to a fixed number — the true distance. Each thin slice contributes a sliver of area under the $v$-$t$ curve, so total distance is the *area under the velocity curve*.` },
        { kind: "formal", md: `This limiting sum is the **definite integral**: $$x = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} v(t)\\,dt = \\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0} \\sum_i v(t_i)\\,\\Delta t.$$ It is exactly the reverse bookkeeping of the derivative: if $F'(t) = f(t)$, then $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} f(t)\\,dt = F(t_2)-F(t_1)$ (the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus). Key building block: $\\int t^n\\,dt = \\frac{t^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$ (for $n \\ne -1$).` },
        { kind: "apply", md: `If $v(t) = 2t$ (m/s), distance from $t=0$ to $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\int_0^3 2t\\,dt = [t^2]_0^3 = 9\\,\\text{m}$ — matching the area of the triangle under the line $v=2t$, base 3, height 6, area $\\tfrac12(3)(6)=9$.` },
        { kind: "transfer", md: `Whenever the exam gives a rate and asks for a total — work from a varying force, charge from a varying current, impulse from a varying force over time — that total is an integral, i.e. "area under the rate curve." When the curve is a simple shape (triangle, rectangle), skip the calculus and just compute the area.` }
