        { kind: "discovery", md: `Work needs the *aligned* component of force — only the part of $\\vec F$ that points along $\\vec d$ does anything, which is $|\\vec F|\\cos\\theta$ resolved along $\\vec d$: candidate 2 for work. Torque needs the opposite: only the part of $\\vec F$ *perpendicular* to the handle produces a twist, and the twist has a definite rotation axis (found by curling fingers from $\\vec r$ to $\\vec F$) — candidate 3 for torque. The two everyday questions ("how much overlap" vs "how much perpendicular turning effect, and about what axis") demand two structurally different products.` },
        { kind: "formal", md: `**Dot product** (scalar result): $$\\vec A\\cdot\\vec B = |\\vec A||\\vec B|\\cos\\theta = A_xB_x+A_yB_y+A_zB_z.$$ **Cross product** (vector result, magnitude $|\\vec A||\\vec B|\\sin\\theta$, direction by the right-hand rule, perpendicular to both): $$\\vec A\\times\\vec B = (A_yB_z-A_zB_y,\\ A_zB_x-A_xB_z,\\ A_xB_y-A_yB_x).$$ Note $\\vec A\\times\\vec B = -\\vec B\\times\\vec A$ (order matters); $\\vec A\\cdot\\vec B = \\vec B\\cdot\\vec A$ (order doesn't).` },
        { kind: "apply", md: `Work: $W = \\vec F\\cdot\\vec d$. If $|\\vec F|=10\\,\\text{N}$, $|\\vec d|=3\\,\\text{m}$, $\\theta=60°$: $W = 10(3)(0.5)=15\\,\\text{J}$. Torque: $\\vec\\tau = \\vec r\\times\\vec F$. If $|\\vec r|=0.2\\,\\text{m}$, $|\\vec F|=50\\,\\text{N}$ perpendicular ($\\theta=90°$): $|\\vec\\tau| = 0.2(50)(1) = 10\\,\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$.` },
        { kind: "transfer", md: `Dot products show up wherever "effective component along a direction" matters: power $P=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec v$, flux $\\Phi = \\vec E\\cdot\\vec A$. Cross products show up wherever rotation, magnetism, or angular momentum appears: $\\vec L = \\vec r\\times\\vec p$, magnetic force $\\vec F = q\\vec v\\times\\vec B$. On the exam, seeing "$\\cos$" in a formula signals a dot product in disguise; seeing "$\\sin$" with a stated rotation axis signals a cross product.` }
